nodecode-dike is a rewrite of Grizaceo's Grizaceo/hermes-dike:

  https://github.com/Grizaceo/hermes-dike
  commit 9780e467ec31d4635eb559e4c09bd49f17e178de

MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Cristóbal Luis Muñoz Rojas (LICENSE).

What was rewritten. The original is a thin Hermes adapter over a Python
verification engine; this cell is that verification, in Common Lisp, with the
statute pack shipped beside it. The citation grammar was carried over: the
article prefix (art., artículo, artículos, arts.), an enumeration of numbers
with its separators (1545, 1546 y 1547), the number words (artículo número
9999), the ordinal forms (artículo primero), the transitory form (art. t. 5),
the square degree between numeral and suffix (39° bis), the Latin suffixes
(bis, ter, quáter, quinquies …) and the letter after a suffix (152 quáter N).
The token normalisation matches: 0006_a => 6-a, t_0005 => t-5. The answer
keeps the original's shape and its Spanish vocabulary: veredicto, detalles
with estado VERIFICADA or FALLO, resolucion, vigencia, estado_texto_historico,
texto_hash, no_verificadas, meta with pack_hash and the counts, and the same
caveats (no substantive certification, no live vigencia, a mention with no
article is not verified at all). The adapter's classification of an unusable
answer into an error envelope is kept too.

What was left out, and why. The original runs a 40,000-line Python engine; five
of its modules are reached by `dike constancia`. This cell does the part a user
sees -- detect the citations, resolve each to a statute, check the article and
its in-force window at the date asked, and report -- and leaves the rest:
- the Motor/NIF studio, the human-in-the-loop gates and the content signing
  (commitment, signature, public key): a signature proves who said it, not that
the article exists, and needs a key this cell has nowhere to keep;
- HITL receipts, the starter-graph edge store, the exports and the SPARQL
  shapes of the pack: none of them is read by `constancia`;
- the .docx and .pdf readers: this cell takes text, or a .md / .txt file;
- the 8,544-article pack's full JSON-LD rendering and its 5 MB tarball: the
  cell ships two TSV tables (one row per article: token, text hash, in-force
  window, retrieval date), 964 KB, read once at first use and checked against
  PACK_HASH in every answer;
- the 159-name alias registry: names come from the pack's own statute titles
  plus the short forms chilean practice writes (cc, cpc, cpp, ct, cot, cpr);
- the original's runtime install and hash re-check of the pack on every call:
  a table that ships inside the cell needs no install, and the cell's own hash
  is the one the answers carry;
- the original's shell-out to a Python subprocess with a filtered environment:
  there is no subprocess here, so there is no environment to filter.

What is untouched. The statute data is the original's chile-base pack, snapshot
af1422d66d1e6eee: 25 statutes, 8,544 articles, each with the SHA-256 of its
text, its in-force window and its retrieval date. It is derived from Ley Chile
-- Biblioteca del Congreso Nacional de Chile (https://nuevo.leychile.cl/),
retrieved 2026-08-18, and it is NOT under the MIT License above: the repository's
LICENSE and corpus/README.md in the original state the terms the package declares
for the legal texts (outside copyright, attribution required), the metadata and
the schema. It is a dated copy, not today's law.
